The effect size of exercise on anxiety in clinical trials is comparable to that of SSRIs — without the side effects or cost.
Even a single 10-minute walk has been shown to reduce anxiety scores measurably; you do not need intense training to see benefits.
Exercise promotes neuroplasticity by increasing BDNF (brain-derived neurotrophic factor), literally building new neural connections that improve resilience to stress.
Occupational studies show workers with regular exercise habits take 27% fewer sick days for stress-related illness than sedentary colleagues.
The mechanism is biological, not motivational — even involuntary exercise (animals forced to run) shows the same cortisol-reducing effect, ruling out placebo.
